<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Czechs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Czechs are a West Slavic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, in the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, Czechs pressed for autonomy comparable to that given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1867, leading to persistent political conflict, especially in Bohemia. Czech nationalism contributed to the empire's dissolution and the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1918.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was a mosaic of peoples: Germans, Hungarians, <b>Czechs</b>, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, Romanians, Croats, Serbs, Slovenes and Italians, with large Jewish communities in Galicia, Bohemia and Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Compromise was a creative answer to an acute problem, but it embedded structural contradictions: it satisfied Hungarian aspirations while leaving the empire's other nationalities, <b>Czechs</b>, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, Slovenes, Croats, Serbs, Romanians, Italians and others, without comparable autonomy, a permanent source of political tension.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1921 census counted about 13.6 million people, of whom <b>Czechs</b> and Slovaks, officially treated as one Czechoslovak nation, formed about two-thirds; there were also more than three million Germans, about 750,000 Hungarians, Ruthenes, Jews and Poles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Finns, <b>Czechs</b>, Hungarians and others turned their languages into vehicles of literature and national pride.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Outside events were catalysts: the changes in Poland and Hungary and the collapse of the East German regime encouraged <b>Czechs</b> and Slovaks to take to the streets, and Western broadcasters such as Radio Free Europe, Voice of America and the BBC World Service carried uncensored news despite jamming.</blockquote><a href="/movement/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Tyl is said to have thought of leaving it out of the play, unsure of its quality, but it soon became the most popular song of Fiddlers' Feast among <b>Czechs</b> who were seeking to revive their identity within the Habsburg monarchy, and quickly became their unofficial patriotic anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kde-domov-muj">Kde domov můj</a></li></ul></section>
